# Hexbeat - sixty-three kits and an Init, voiced against the debug build.
#
# A drum patch is not a sound, it is thirteen sounds and the balance between
# them, so these are named for the room they belong in rather than for the
# knob that made them. What separates them is mostly three things: how long
# things ring, where the metal sits, and which of the small voices - rim,
# clave, cowbell, clap - is carrying the pattern instead of decorating it.
#
# The internal balance is the part to read - `audition bank Hexbeat` prints
# each voice against the loudest in its own kit. A kit whose hat is six
# decibels under its kick is a kit you never hear the hats in.
#
# **The eleven kits this replaces all measured -0.0 dB peak.** Not because
# they were balanced: the machine summed thirteen voices straight into a tanh
# with nothing before it, so every kit was clamped flat against the ceiling
# and the old header wrote that up as "within a tenth of a decibel, which is
# the number that matters for a kit". It was the number that mattered for the
# clipper. `kDrive` now sets how hard the voices reach it - a single hit
# stays straight, a whole kit on one beat bends - and a `volume` knob, which
# this machine did not have at all, carries the levelling.
#
# Families in tab order, plainest to strangest within each: classic 10 (Init
# first), dry 8, room 8, dirty 8, metal 8, electro 8, perc 8, odd 6. Every
# kit but Init carries eight named macros. The room kits but Parlour send to
# the song's reverb, Canyon to its delay too, and the dirty kits from Worn on
# are driven, most through an insert or two. Elsewhere effects are only where
# the kit needs them: Gleam, Gongs and Timbales send to the reverb, Throb to
# the delay, Servo and Zapper echo through their own delay, Gleam has a
# chorus, Zapper a phaser, Molasses a tape and Busted and Glitchy a crusher.
#
# Every kit peaks at about -11 dB, like Init.
#
# Values are in each parameter's own units; `audition params Hexbeat` lists
# them.

machine Hexbeat
role    beat

patch Init family=classic

patch Squared family=classic
  # The workhorse: the defaults pulled into focus. A kick with enough sweep
  # to speak on a small speaker, a snare that is half noise, hats short
  # enough to sit under a sixteenth pattern.
  set macro1 "Tighter"
  set macro2 "Pitch"
  set macro3 "Snap"
  set macro4 "Hats"
  set macro5 "Weight"
  set macro6 "Clap"
  set macro7 "Push"
  set macro8 "Small"
  kick_tune      54 Hz
  kick_decay     330 ms
  kick_punch     0.58
  snare_decay    190 ms
  snare_snappy   0.55
  snare_tone     2400 Hz
  tom_decay      300 ms
  hat_closed_decay 44 ms
  hat_open_decay 420 ms
